; Infantryman, ~1.55m to the top of the helmet, built from primitives. Origin
; at the feet. Everything above the waist hangs off Spine, which the player
; pitches so the weapon tracks where you are aiming. WeaponSocket is the
; attachment point -- swapping weapons means reparenting a different scene onto
; it.
;
; The shapes are round because a man is: the chest, waist, hips and skull are
; ovals rather than boxes, and the joints are covered by the shoulder caps so
; the arms grow out of the body instead of floating beside it. What is left
; boxy is what is boxy on the real thing -- pouches, the pack, boots.
;
; The kit is doing as much work as the anatomy. A belt, cross straps, two ammo
; pouches, a canteen, a bayonet and a slung pack are what make a shape at a
; hundred yards read as a soldier rather than as a person out for a walk, and
; they break up the silhouette so a man against a hedgerow is not one flat
; olive blob.
;
; Every anchor the rest of the game reaches for is where it always was: hips at
; y=0.73, knees a 0.315 segment below, Spine at 0.84, the arm rig's shoulders at
; +-0.21 from it, and the bottom of the skull on the 1.32 headshot line.
